  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
